      "Language": "eng",
      "Abstract": {"AbstractText": [
        {
          "NlmCategory": "BACKGROUND",
          "Label": "CONTEXT",
          "content": "In Russia, where health status has deteriorated since the late 1960s, asthma is a growing medical and public health problem."
        },
        {
          "NlmCategory": "OBJECTIVE",
          "Label": "OBJECTIVE",
          "content": "To create a model for improving care and outcomes of patients with asthma through altering the culture of health care, physician behavior, and public policy in a Russian community."
        },
        {
          "NlmCategory": "METHODS",
          "Label": "DESIGN",
          "content": "A 6-month, nonrandomized, before-and-after intervention evaluation."
        },
        {
          "NlmCategory": "METHODS",
          "Label": "SETTING",
          "content": "Outpatients of Medical-Sanitary Unit No. 50, the central health authority in Sarov, Russia, a \"closed\" nuclear city."
        },
        {
          "NlmCategory": "METHODS",
          "Label": "PARTICIPANTS",
          "content": "A consecutive sample of 85 adult patients with severe-persistent or moderate-persistent asthma."
        },
        {
          "NlmCategory": "METHODS",
          "Label": "INTERVENTIONS",
          "content": "A comprehensive asthma-care program that emphasized patient education and self-management with treatment based on internationally accepted guidelines modified for local resources."
        },
        {
          "NlmCategory": "METHODS",
          "Label": "MAIN OUTCOME MEASURES",
          "content": "Missed work or school, patients requiring emergency department visits, number of patients hospitalized, daytime symptoms, nighttime symptoms, rescue inhaler use, patient satisfaction, and FEV(1)."
        },
        {
          "NlmCategory": "RESULTS",
          "Label": "RESULTS",
          "content": "After 6 months, significant reductions were observed in the proportion of patients missing work or school (1.2% vs 11.8%), emergency department visits (4.8% vs 15.7%), hospitalizations (0% vs 9.4%), daily symptoms (47.1% vs 65.9%), and nightly symptoms (14.1% vs 37.6%). Patient satisfaction with asthma control (81.2% vs 31.8%) and average level of FEV(1) (84.0% vs 72.4%) significantly increased from baseline."
        },
        {
          "NlmCategory": "CONCLUSIONS",
          "Label": "CONCLUSIONS",
          "content": "The model for changing asthma management in this Russian community was effective in improving asthma outcomes and offers a reproducible paradigm approach for improving chronic illness care."
        }
      ]},
